/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  swro01_campoDalias -- A TWO-PASS SEAWATER DESALINATION PLANT FROM A
  MANUFACTURER'S PUBLIC CASE STUDY (DEV.md 4c C194).

  SOURCE.  Toray Industries, "Seawater Desalination Using Toray's RO Membranes
  to Support a Thriving Agricultural Economy" (Campo de Dalias Desalination
  Plant, Almeria), MC-CS-CAMPODEDALIAS-1910, 2019: two pages, a MANUFACTURER'S
  document about its own membranes.  Every performance figure in it is
  Toray's claim "based on manufacturer's test conditions"; none is a
  measurement of this case.  It gives: an open intake by a 1.6 km subsea
  pipeline; pretreatment by 40 dual-media filters (glass-fibre-reinforced
  polyester) then 5-micron cartridges (99.6 % particulate removal); a two-pass
  reverse osmosis of six skids each, the first pass TM820E-400 seawater
  elements (skid 233 vessels of 7 elements, "233 (7M)"; 37 m2, 28.3 m3/d,
  99.75 % NaCl and 91 % boron at pH 8; "47.2 % system recovery"), the second
  pass TM720C-430 brackish elements (54:18 vessels of 7 elements; 40 m2,
  33.3 m3/d, 99.2 % NaCl and 95 % boron at pH 10; "84-90 %" recovery); the
  second pass's concentrate recycled to the first; an isobaric energy-recovery
  system of 95 % efficiency (DWEER); post-treatment by remineralisation and
  disinfection; 98 664 m3/day; the feed analysis (its Table 2); a permeate
  target of TDS < 400 mg/L and boron < 0.5 mg/L.  NOT in it: a site plan,
  vessel and pump counts beyond the skid designs, pump sizes, energy per m3,
  costs, the temperature of operation (14-23 C is the feed's range).

  WHAT THIS CASE TAKES FROM IT, AND WHAT IT ADDS.
    From the document (origin literature): the 98 664 m3/day; the six skids and
    their vessel counts (1398 vessels in the first pass, 324 + 108 in the
    second, 7 elements each); the elements' areas, flows and rejections; the
    47.2 % first-pass recovery and the second pass's 84-90 % (87 % chosen);
    the 95 % energy-recovery efficiency; the recycle; the feed analysis.
    The document's two internal figures for the product differ slightly
    (98 664 m3/day against 6 skids x 16 442 = 98 652): the case uses 98 664.
    ASSUMED by the case, each with its reason where it stands: the test
    conditions of the manufacturer's figures; the membranes' water
    permeances (derived from them); the elements' geometry; the temperature
    (25 C, 2 K above the document's feed range -- the module has no
    temperature correction of A_w); pump efficiencies; the device's mixing and
    leakage; every hold time and tank; the whole plot plan.
    NOT MODELLED: the particulate removal of the filters (no solids), the
    filters' own pressure drop, the pH and the caustic dosing between the
    passes, the remineralisation and disinfection chemistry, the real
    device's piston hydraulics, fouling and the elements' ageing.

  THE SALTS ARE ONE LUMPED SOLUTE.  The document's Table 2 lists twelve ions
  (TDS 38 697 mg/L).  The module's rated-test inversion needs a salt, not
  ions, so the ions are replaced by NaCl at the SAME OSMOTIC PRESSURE: the
  ions' 1235.9 mmol/L give 617.9 mmol/L NaCl = 36.11 g/L, against the
  38 697 mg/L of actual TDS (the lump weighs 6.7 % less than the ions; the
  permeate TDS quoted below, as NaCl, reads about 7 % lower than a real TDS).
  Boron (5.3 mg/L as B) is a second lumped solute.

  THE FIGURES THE DOCUMENT CALLS "FEED PRESSURE" ARE THE TEST PRESSURES, AND
  THE PLANT'S ARE SOLVED.  Table 1 prints 5.52 MPa (800 psi) and 1.03 MPa
  (150 psi) beside the elements' flow and rejection, which are test figures.
  The case takes them as the rated tests' pressures.  They cannot be the
  plant's: a 47.2 % recovery of this seawater leaves a concentrate at
  63.5 g/L NaCl-equivalent (about 53 bar of osmotic pressure), which 55.2 bar
  cannot push past; the first pass here needs 58.6 bar (HP pump and booster
  discharge) for 47.2 %.  The second pass runs at 10.74 bar, not 10.3, which
  puts its recovery at 87 % (the document's 84-90 %) with the first pass's
  permeate: at 10.3 bar the same array recovers 94.8 %.

  WHAT THE RUN SAYS (2026-11-02, boron split by the pH, C198; the golden is the authority):
      product                4111.2 m3/h = 98 668.7 m3/day (the document's 98 664)
      intake                 9461.3 m3/h; recovery of the intake 43.5 %
      first pass             47.20 % recovery (the document's 47.2 %), 13.05
                             LMH average flux on 362 082 m2, feed 10 011 m3/h at
                             58.59 bar, brine 63.5 g/L NaCl-eq at 57.9 bar,
                             permeate 109.6 mg/L NaCl-eq, boron 0.457 mg/L
      second pass            87.0 % recovery, 33.99 LMH on 120 960 m2 (the
                             element's rated 34.7), concentrate 614 m3/h at
                             0.83 g/L, boron 3.2 mg/L, recycled to the first
      product                1.9 mg/L NaCl-eq (the document's target < 400
                             mg/L TDS), boron 0.043 mg/L (< 0.5)
      energy recovery        flows balanced to 1e-5; 54.1 bar recovered on
                             5209 m3/h, 7.76 MW hydraulic; the feed's
                             salinity up 1.8 %
      pump shaft powers      HP pump 9185 kW, booster 437 kW, second-pass
                             pump 1604 kW, intake 326 kW: 11 552 kW, i.e.
                             2.81 kWh per m3 of product (shaft only: no
                             motor losses, no pretreatment or post-treatment
                             loads, no product delivery)

  BORON IS TWO SPECIES SPLIT BY THE pH (DEV.md 4c C198, Stage 1).  Boron is
  the reason for the second pass and for the caustic between the passes:
  boric acid (neutral, small enough to pass a seawater membrane) is
  converted at pH 10 into borate (an anion, rejected).  Each module declares
  `aqueousSplit {}`: the apparent boron is carried as H3BO3 and BOH4, the
  base fraction alpha = 1/(1 + 10^(pKa - pH)) is asked of the PACKAGE (the
  curated constant data/standards/chemistry/BOH4-formation.dat, pKa 9.24 with
  its van't Hoff enthalpy, and this case's declared aqueous activity model,
  Davies, for gamma and a_w) at the module inlet's bulk and the DECLARED pH,
  and each form has its own intrinsic rejection.  Alpha is 0.108 at the first
  pass (pH 8.2, I = 0.59 mol/kg) and 0.858 at the second (pH 10).
  THE PERMEANCES come from the sheet's rated tests: ONE boron test per
  element (91 % at pH 8; 95 % at pH 10, `boronRef` in the two records) is
  inverted through the module's own film and, at the test's own alpha,
  determines the ACID's rejection; the BASE's is a DECLARED ASSUMPTION (the
  element's own NaCl rejection: an anion rejected like the salt) because one
  test cannot identify two permeances.  TWO tests of one element at two pH
  would give both (`rejection { tests ( id1 id2 ) }`; the gate runs that form
  on a fixture), and the engine refuses a single test offered as two or two
  at the same pH.  The result is R_acid 0.932 and R_base 0.998 for the
  first-pass element, 0.790 and 0.994 for the second-pass one.
  THE LIMITS, announced on every run: THE pH IS A DECLARED LABEL of each
  module (8.2, Table 2; 10, Table 1), not a state -- no stream carries a pH,
  and the caustic dosing that would set the second pass's pH is NOT a unit
  (Stage 2); the split is FROZEN at the inlet's pH along the channel; both
  forms share the one film coefficient; Davies is outside its own ~0.5 mol/kg
  band at seawater strength, so alpha at the first pass is indicative (the
  apparent pKa it gives, 9.12, is NOT Dickson's conditional seawater constant,
  8.60 on the total scale -- the comparison is printed by the gate, never
  tuned).  What the case now shows: lower the second pass's declared pH from 10
  to 9 and the product's boron rises from 0.04 to 0.14 mg/L, as alpha predicts.
  A finding about the probe: the boron test is water plus 5 ppm boron at the
  test's pressure, with no osmotic pressure to oppose it, so it runs at 19 %
  recovery instead of the test's 8-15 %; the derived effective rejection
  (0.9357, 0.9642) is that of the probe.  And boron is a solute of the van't
  Hoff osmotic pressure (`dissociation 1`): the second pass's feed is so dilute
  that boron is about 1 % of its osmotic pressure, so the product moved by
  0.004 % when boron's rejection changed from the lumped to the split form (the
  NaCl-side rows of the golden moved for that reason alone).

  THE LOOP IS A KNIFE-EDGE, AND 0/ IS A CONVERGED STATE.  The exchanger
  returns 99 % of the brine's volume to the membrane feed, so the first pass
  is a loop of gain near one (membrane27 shows it), and the second pass's
  concentrate is a second tear.  A cold start from a rough guess converges,
  with this Wegstein, to a SPURIOUS fixed point (all water circulating, no
  permeate); 0/ therefore holds the converged state of a previous run and the
  case restarts from it in two iterations.  Changing a pressure or a flow
  moves the answer: re-seed from converged/ or tune the split fraction
  (0.4791) to balance the exchanger.  The intake flow, the split, the two
  pass pressures and the first-pass pressure were solved together for the
  product, 47.2 %, 87 % and a balanced exchanger.

  SIX TRAINS (DEV.md 4c C199).  The document's six skids are six identical
  trains in parallel, so this flowsheet carries their SUM (`vessels 1398` is
  6 x 233, and the pumps and the exchanger carry six times a train's flow); the
  physics is the same and is solved once.  The sizing is per train:
  system/postDict declares `trains { count 6; }` and yields train1 .. train6 for
  each pump, exchanger array and vessel bank.

  THE PLOT PLAN (system/layoutDict) is Choupo's design of a plant of this
  scale up to the fence, NOT the real site: the document gives no site plan.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

tearStreams ( brine  conc2 );

units
(
    //  ---- the intake and the pretreatment ---------------------------------
    {
        name        IntakeChamber;
        type        mixer;                      // a one-inlet junction: the intake's wet well, where the subsea line ends -- a node the plot plan can route the line to
        inputs      ( seawater );
        outputs     ( s0 );
        operation   { T 298.15 K; }
    }
    {
        name        IntakePump;
        type        pump;
        in          s0;
        outputs     ( s1 );
        operation
        {
            P_out     2.0 bar;                  // ASSUMED: lifts the 1.6 km subsea line's water through the filters to the HP pump's suction; the filters' own pressure drops are NOT modelled (no valve/pipe unit prices on this component set's enthalpy)
            eta       0.80;                     // ASSUMED
        }
    }
    {
        name        MediaFilters;
        type        storageTank;                // PLACEHOLDER: a pass-through with a hold time; the filtration is NOT modelled
        in          s1;
        outputs     ( s2 );
        operation   { residenceTime 5 min;  fillFraction 0.85; }    // ASSUMED empty-bed contact time of 40 dual-media filters in GRP (the document: 40 units, glass-fibre-reinforced polyester); the 99.6 % particulate removal is the manufacturer's figure and has no solids to act on here
    }
    {
        name        CartridgeFilters;
        type        storageTank;                // PLACEHOLDER, as above
        in          s2;
        outputs     ( s3 );
        operation   { residenceTime 0.5 min;  fillFraction 0.85; }  // ASSUMED; the document: 5 micron cartridges
    }
    {
        name        FeedMix;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( s3  conc2 );                // the pretreated seawater and the second pass's concentrate
        outputs     ( feed1 );
        operation   { T 298.15 K; }
    }

    //  ---- the first pass: HP pump + energy recovery + 1398 vessels --------
    {
        name        Split;
        type        splitter;
        in          feed1;
        outputs     ( toHPpump  toPX );
        operation   { fractions ( 0.479110  0.520890 ); }
    }
    {
        name        HPpump;
        type        pump;
        in          toHPpump;
        outputs     ( hpFeed );
        operation
        {
            P_out     58.589 bar;
            eta       0.82;                     // ASSUMED
        }
    }
    {
        name        PX;
        type        pressureExchanger;
        inputs      ( brine  toPX );
        outputs     ( pxHPout  spentBrine );
        operation
        {
            efficiency      0.95;               // the document: "isobaric energy recovery system ... 95 % efficiency"
            mixing          0.02;               // ASSUMED
            leakage         0.01;               // ASSUMED
            P_LPout         1.3 bar;            // ASSUMED: the outfall line
            targetPressure  58.589 bar;
            source          "efficiency 0.95: Toray case study MC-CS-CAMPODEDALIAS-1910 (DWEER, 95 %); mixing and leakage: ASSUMED, low-end values of Stover, Desalination 203 (2007) 168-175, for a device the document does not describe further; the rotary unit of this engine stands in for the document's piston-type work exchanger";
        }
    }
    {
        name        Booster;
        type        pump;
        in          pxHPout;
        outputs     ( boosted );
        operation
        {
            P_out     58.589 bar;
            eta       0.80;                     // ASSUMED
        }
    }
    {
        name        Join;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( hpFeed  boosted );
        outputs     ( roFeed );
        operation   { T 298.15 K; }
    }
    {
        name        RO1;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          roFeed;
        outputs     ( brine  perm1 );
        operation
        {
            module           TM820E-400;        // constant/assets/, case-local
            vessels          1398;              // 6 skids x 233 vessels (the document: "233 (7M)")
            elements         7;                 // "7M": seven elements per vessel
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            rho_feed         1025;              // kg/m3, ASSUMED: seawater near 20 C
            nNodes           10;
            aqueousSplit
            {
                component   boron;
                acid        H3BO3;
                base        BOH4;
                pH          8.2;
                pHSource    "Toray case study MC-CS-CAMPODEDALIAS-1910, Table 2 (the seawater analysis: pH 8.2); the pH of the feed is a DECLARED label of this module (Stage 1: no stream carries a pH)";
                rejection
                {
                    acid  ( fromRatedTest boronRef );    // the acid from the sheet's 91 % at pH 8 (Table 1)
                    base  ( sameAs NaCl );              // ASSUMED: borate is an anion and is rejected like the salt (the module's own NaCl intrinsic rejection); the data sheet states one boron test per element, which cannot identify two permeances
                }
            }
            transport        constantRejection;
            transportParameters
            {
                rejection
                {
                    NaCl  ( fromRatedTest naclRef );
                    boron ( fromAqueousSplit );
                }
                source  "DERIVED from the Toray case study's observed rejections (NaCl 99.75 %, boron 91 % at pH 8; MC-CS-CAMPODEDALIAS-1910, Table 1) by inverting two ASSUMED standard tests with the module's own film; boron is the apparent total of boric acid and borate, split by the pH of the aqueousSplit block (see header)";
            }
            massTransfer
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;
                viscosity      1.0e-3;          // Pa.s, ASSUMED seawater near 20 C
                diffusivity    1.5e-9;          // m2/s, ASSUMED: one value serves both solutes (NaCl at seawater strength)
            }
            pressureDrop
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;
            }
            polarisation
            {
                suctionCorrection  GeraldesAfonso2006;
                ionCoupling        none;        // lumped solutes: nothing to couple
            }
        }
    }

    //  ---- the second pass: pH raised, two stages, 84-90 % recovery --------
    //  The caustic dosing that lifts the first pass's permeate to pH 10 is a
    //  DECLARED PLACEHOLDER, not a unit: no stream carries a pH here, and the
    //  second pass's boron rejection is read from the pH 10 rated test.
    {
        name        Pass2Pump;
        type        pump;
        in          perm1;
        outputs     ( feed2 );
        operation
        {
            P_out     10.7447 bar;                 // SOLVED: 87 % second-pass recovery (the document's 1.03 MPa is the test pressure)
            eta       0.80;                     // ASSUMED
        }
    }
    {
        name        RO2a;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          feed2;
        outputs     ( conc2a  perm2a );
        operation
        {
            module           TM720C-430;
            vessels          324;               // 6 skids x 54 vessels (the document: "54:18 (7M)", first stage)
            elements         7;
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            nNodes           10;
            aqueousSplit
            {
                component   boron;
                acid        H3BO3;
                base        BOH4;
                pH          10;
                pHSource    "DECLARED: the case study states the second pass at pH 10 (Table 1, boron rejection 95 % at pH 10) and no dosing; the caustic that sets this pH is Stage 2 and is NOT modelled -- the pH is a label of this module";
                rejection
                {
                    acid  ( fromRatedTest boronRef );    // the acid from the sheet's 95 % at pH 10 (Table 1)
                    base  ( sameAs NaCl );              // ASSUMED: borate is an anion and is rejected like the salt (the module's own NaCl intrinsic rejection); the data sheet states one boron test per element, which cannot identify two permeances
                }
            }
            transport        constantRejection;
            transportParameters
            {
                rejection
                {
                    NaCl  ( fromRatedTest naclRef );
                    boron ( fromAqueousSplit );
                }
                source  "DERIVED from the Toray case study's observed rejections (NaCl 99.2 %, boron 95 % at pH 10; MC-CS-CAMPODEDALIAS-1910, Table 1) by inverting two ASSUMED standard tests; boron the apparent total of boric acid and borate, split by the pH of the aqueousSplit block (see header)";
            }
            massTransfer
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;
                viscosity      1.0e-3;
                diffusivity    1.5e-9;
            }
            pressureDrop     { model SchockMiquel; }
            polarisation
            {
                suctionCorrection  GeraldesAfonso2006;
                ionCoupling        none;
            }
        }
    }
    {
        name        RO2b;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          conc2a;
        outputs     ( conc2  perm2b );
        operation
        {
            module           TM720C-430;
            vessels          108;               // 6 skids x 18 vessels (the document: "54:18", second stage)
            elements         7;
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            nNodes           10;
            aqueousSplit
            {
                component   boron;
                acid        H3BO3;
                base        BOH4;
                pH          10;
                pHSource    "DECLARED: the case study states the second pass at pH 10 (Table 1, boron rejection 95 % at pH 10) and no dosing; the caustic that sets this pH is Stage 2 and is NOT modelled -- the pH is a label of this module";
                rejection
                {
                    acid  ( fromRatedTest boronRef );    // the acid from the sheet's 95 % at pH 10 (Table 1)
                    base  ( sameAs NaCl );              // ASSUMED: borate is an anion and is rejected like the salt (the module's own NaCl intrinsic rejection); the data sheet states one boron test per element, which cannot identify two permeances
                }
            }
            transport        constantRejection;
            transportParameters
            {
                rejection
                {
                    NaCl  ( fromRatedTest naclRef );
                    boron ( fromAqueousSplit );
                }
                source  "as RO2a";
            }
            massTransfer
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;
                viscosity      1.0e-3;
                diffusivity    1.5e-9;
            }
            pressureDrop     { model SchockMiquel; }
            polarisation
            {
                suctionCorrection  GeraldesAfonso2006;
                ionCoupling        none;
            }
        }
    }
    {
        name        ProductMix;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( perm2a  perm2b );
        outputs     ( perm2 );
        operation   { T 298.15 K; }
    }
    //  ---- post-treatment: DECLARED PLACEHOLDERS, then the product store ---
    {
        name        Remineralisation;
        type        storageTank;
        in          perm2;
        outputs     ( remin );
        operation   { residenceTime 0.25 h;  fillFraction 0.85; }   // PLACEHOLDER: a contact tank; the lime/CO2 dosing is NOT modelled
    }
    {
        name        Disinfection;
        type        storageTank;
        in          remin;
        outputs     ( disinfected );
        operation   { residenceTime 0.5 h;  fillFraction 0.85; }   // PLACEHOLDER: a contact tank; the chlorine dosing is NOT modelled
    }
    {
        name        ProductTank;
        type        storageTank;
        in          disinfected;
        outputs     ( product );
        operation   { residenceTime 6 h;  fillFraction 0.85; }      // ASSUMED: six hours of product
    }
);
